The rise of modern web applications has significantly transformed the educational technology landscape, including the field of Speech-Language Pathology (SLP). However, many existing solutions are broad, large-scale platforms designed with a one-size-fits-all approach. Advancements such as real-time feedback powered by generative AI, secure-by-design data management, and intuitive, automated administrative tools represent critical innovations that can enhance the quality of care and drive technological adoption among Speech-Language Pathologists.
As web applications are introduced, the personalization and tailoring of solutions for Speech Sound Disorders (SSDs) is becoming more and more possible. Real-time feedback can assist by helping SLPs quickly identify SSDs in a manner not seen before. As Roper et al. (2022) indicate, “Telehealth has existed since at least the 1960s, and has included SLP services since at least the mid-1970s” (p. 528), which shows that service at a distance has been an option for a long time; yet there are low adoption rates for remote therapy.
Applications like Sonde Health use AI to analyze speech for general health issues and monitoring, but are not geared towards SSDs. On the other hand, the Speechelo application gives clients examples of correct speech. However it lacks the data governance, and interactivity that is necessary for learning as research in this project has shown.
Currently, there are no web applications that comprehensively integrate all of the following components: modern web application frameworks, real-time generative AI analysis of speech, robust data governance and privacy protection, and advanced administrative automation.

Related Work
Modern interactive features within any application are necessary for analysis in the Speech Language Pathology field, specifically when treating younger patients. Heyman (2020) highlights that “interactive features in an app can draw the child’s attention to particular content. This can be used to provide support and scaffold the learning” (p. 307). This classic pedagogical approach improves their ability to process speech and represent information cognitively. Furthermore, Heyman (2020) emphasizes the value of external motivation, stating, “The use of external rewards is particularly beneficial to increase motivation and performance on tasks that have low initial interest” (p. 308), thus reinforcing the importance of maintaining learner engagement through app features. It is a good idea to use rewards, such as game tokens, inside of an application so that the clients can remain engaged with the process. The product can be extremely educational and beneficial, but learning results will not be achieved if the client is not stimulated to keep using it. Though this principle is usually applied to games, it is possible to modify speech tests to be more like games and keep the patient more stimulated.
One concern with using web applications is “in order to effectively use apps for language intervention, it is necessary to consider the potential impact that the use of apps has on processing of information and working memory” (Heyman, N. 2020, p.307). Because learning is done by the human brain, it is important to keep the application simple. The goal is that the application should stimulate the client just enough to keep learning, but not to override the process with too much gamification or distractions. In other words, efficient learning requires balance achieved between stimulation and disciplined focus.
Another benefit of mobile technologies is they have demonstrated their ability to extend the reach of speech therapy services. Benedon (2018) points out that “mobile technologies have been found to increase autonomy, improve productivity, enhance interprofessional communication, and permit efficient access to occupation-relevant data in the workplace.” As a result of the autonomy that these applications provide, those who are in more impoverished areas can gain an equitable access to the same resources as those in more developed areas. This means that the divide between those more and less fortunate should shrink with time. Benedon, T. A. (2018) expects “increased rates of use of mobile tools to support administration of care and patient education in healthcare settings”. This is important for creating scalable solutions that are equitable as well, as the advent of mobile phones and smartphones has opened up the world of the internet for many people around the world. It is amazing that a user in one country has access to a similar, if not identical, set of applications as those in another country. This can lead to a great reach for modern diagnostic web applications, and an equitable result in providing care.
There are many challenges that come with incorporating such applications into the broader educational system however.  SLPs themselves have a hard time adopting these technologies. “They are faced with the task of constantly adapting to a changing field and finding their own role and identity in the midst of this transformation” (Szabó et al, 2022). Whether it's a fear of being replaced, or simply lack of familiarity with emerging technologies, many research papers echo the aforementioned quote in that SLPs are uncomfortable using web applications. Even though the benefits of using modern technology have been presented, research “clearly shows that the knowledge about the potential advantages of using apps is not sufficient to use them” (Szabó et al, 2022, p.388). There needs to be more work in convincing SLPs how to use these new applications and technologies. 
 One solution to these issues is to offer an open-source, community based application and to continue maintenance: “Lack of app maintenance is reportedly a major reason for the failure of many apps” (Lin et al, 2022, p.11). Many SLPs reported that the wide ecosystem of applications, and specifically their tendency to become obsolete because the application developer loses interest in maintaining them, is a huge reason for why they do not adopt them. Another solution is to involve SLPs, as well as their clients, in the application design process. Roper, et al, 2022 state that “once developed, at least to a pilot level, researchers often assess an intervention for efficacy before consulting end-users as part of understanding feasibility and acceptability of the intervention”. This means that the clinician is actually doing the design work themselves and it is not a team effort as it should be between developers, clinicians, and clients. Using a community-based approach, offering a point for discussion and feature improvements in an open-source forum, could alleviate some of these flaws with the existing design process.
Perhaps the biggest concern that arises frequently is the ability to protect client’s confidential data. “Data privacy concerns are well reported among health-related apps and digital tools, especially considering the vast amounts of sensitive health-related and personal information that can be collected through such means” (Lin et al, 2022, p.11). Because web applications are a relatively new technology and tend to be less regulated than more traditional therapy options, both clinicians and clients are concerned about where their data is going and if it is being adequately protected and rightfully so. Legislation such as the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (HIPAA) was enacted to ensure privacy, security, and accountability for health care. Web applications must adhere to certain legislation, and ethical standards, if they are to gain better traction with speech language professionals.
The addition of a product that utilizes care and concern for privacy, specifically encryption techniques, is very important. By ensuring a password-protected portal exists, and modern web security practices are employed, it is less likely that hackers can access private data. Even if they do, encrypting test data and client information can alleviate concerns. Listen has modern web security protocols and encryption. Security must be prioritized throughout development, as multiple studies list it as a primary stakeholder concern.
Finally, emerging AI and ML techniques are already shaking up most industries they touch. ChatGPT, Gemini, and DeepSeek to name a few have changed the technology landscape all across the world. Combining these techniques with ASR, tools have the ability to change the way SLPs provide therapy and some have already begun researching this. Some products being developed currently use “techniques such as machine learning, deep learning, signal processing, and linguistics to analyze and understand human speech. ASR technology can potentially be used in speech therapy to support individuals living with SSD” (Deka et al, 2025, p.2).  The ways that AI and ML can help specifically are “understanding spoken language, identifying objects and patterns, making decisions, and solving problems” (Deka et al, 2025, p.2). This type of deep understanding has already been seen in the diagnostic field, where some models are predicting cancer at a more efficient rate than human doctors. In fact, SLP applications using AI and ML have been proven to be more efficient as well: “caregivers were lenient in their evaluation or had difficulty in identifying mispronunciations. In contrast, the automated algorithm exhibits superior ability to detect mispronunciations compared to correct pronunciations” (Deka et al, 2025, p.17). In some cases, caregivers or SLPs applying therapy are more lenient and forgiving whereas automated algorithms are more objective.
There have been studies specifically done with ASR and combining it with ML neural networks. “The words spoken by the client in the microphone are processed by a computer program that first extracts the spoken text features. These extracted features are then compared with the trained patterns. Natural language processing was used for the text-to-speech system” (Attwell et al, 2022, p.16). In this study, Attwell analyzed a ML model which analyzed words spoken into a microphone by a client and compared them to a trained pattern. Five studies were conducted, and the most successful one “discussed the use of a fuzzy logic algorithm that assigns weight to the level of the speech disorder” (Attwell et al, 2022, p.17). 
One of the most widely used tools among speech-language pathologists (SLPs) today is SLP Toolkit (https://www.slptoolkit.com), a web-based platform that offers a comprehensive suite of resources including screening instruments, progress monitoring tools, data collection systems, goal-setting frameworks, and report generation features. While SLP Toolkit effectively supports administrative and organizational tasks, it remains primarily a management utility rather than a treatment platform. It does not leverage emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence (AI), automated speech analysis, or adaptive intervention recommendations, which could significantly enhance the platform’s utility by supporting both clinical decision-making and direct therapeutic engagement. As the field increasingly embraces digital transformation, modernization efforts could position tools like SLP Toolkit at the forefront of evidence-based, technology-enhanced speech therapy.

Our solution: Listen.ai
Building on the limitations observed in existing solutions like SLP Toolkit, Listen.ai seeks to address these gaps through near real-time speech analysis and AI-generated feedback, supported by a secure database for data persistence and reporting capabilities all in an open-source solution. It features a modern, intuitive front-end that integrates standardized Speech-Language Pathology (SLP) assessments, including images and prompts designed to facilitate learning. Patients record their pronunciation, which is subsequently analyzed and transmitted to a generative AI model, such as DeepSeek or ChatGPT, for further evaluation. The resulting feedback is returned to the front-end, where it can be easily copied and incorporated by the SLP or patient into administrative documentation or billing reports.
As of this writing, Listen includes one implemented assessment focused on the articulation of the "/s/" sound. The assessment offers word selections featuring the target sound in initial, medial, and final word positions. The objective is to compare the user's pronunciation to the expected standard using AI-driven analysis and to deliver real-time feedback. This feedback can be easily copied by the SLP for inclusion in clinical reports, thereby reducing administrative workload and enabling greater focus on patient care.
The database layer is implemented using Postgres for persisting data that is recorded, so that an individual progress can be tracked securely. As of writing, encryption has been implemented on the passwords of registered users using BCrypt (https://bcrypt.online/). Future implementations of Listen will encrypt any user data, such as patient PII as well as their audio samples. Currently, the audio samples of tests are being stored as BLOBs (https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/sql/Blob.html).
Most importantly, Listen.ai is open-source, meaning its development is freely accessible and open to contributions from anyone. Open-source development encourages transparency, collaboration, and continuous improvement, allowing a community of developers, researchers, and end-users to shape the application to better meet real-world needs. As discussed in the “Related Work” section, one of the major reasons existing applications have struggled to gain traction with SLPs is the lack of meaningful integration with their workflows during the design process. By being open-source, Listen.ai invites direct input from SLPs and other stakeholders, ensuring that the tool evolves in ways that are genuinely useful and aligned with professional practice.


