Methodology and Results/Limitations
Listen.ai has not yet undergone formal evaluation with a representative sample of practicing speech-language pathologists (SLPs). Preliminary anecdotal testing has been conducted with the author’s spouse, a licensed SLP, and several of her colleagues. Their informal feedback has been instrumental in guiding early-stage development by highlighting both strengths and areas in need of improvement. However, anecdotal evidence, while useful for preliminary insights, lacks the methodological rigor necessary to establish the tool’s efficacy and generalizability. Formal validation through structured usability studies and clinical trials is essential to ensure that Listen.ai meets the professional standards required for clinical practice. This is the biggest limitation of the results of this research and development so far.
Initial anecdotal feedback has been overwhelmingly positive. Informal surveys of participating SLPs reported an estimated average time savings of approximately 70%, primarily related to administrative tasks. Respondents identified report writing, test scoring, and the creation of testing materials as the most time-consuming components of their workflow. Listen.ai was noted to significantly alleviate these burdens by automating key processes, including the administration of standardized speech assessments, the generation of clinical reports, and the scoring and analysis of test results, thereby reducing cognitive load and freeing up time for direct patient care.
Survey respondents expressed a desire for a broader selection of speech assessments, noting that the current articulation test alone was insufficient to fully evaluate the tool’s capabilities. While participants found the output of the generative AI component to be impressive, they also indicated that the level of accuracy did not yet meet clinical expectations. It was suggested that improvements in model selection and prompt engineering could enhance the precision and reliability of the AI-generated results, further increasing the tool’s clinical utility.
Conclusion and Future Work
As technology evolves, SLPs, developers, and clients must work together to harness its potential while safeguarding ethical concerns. With thoughtful design, applications like Listen.ai can revolutionize speech therapy for future generations."
Future work will prioritize systematic evaluation protocols to assess the application's usability, effectiveness, and integration within real-world therapeutic contexts. The next iteration of Listen.ai should:
- Conduct formal usability testing with 10+ practicing SLPs.
- Expand speech assessments beyond the /s/ sound.
- Implement encryption for patient PII and audio samples.

